1
00:00:03,300 --> 00:00:05,557
A pipeline starts with a source.

2
00:00:05,557 --> 00:00:08,944
Add a data source opens the data sets you may read:

3
00:00:08,944 --> 00:00:14,290
the content items of each content type, the users, and the results of saved queries.

4
00:00:15,349 --> 00:00:17,970
Let's add the Blog Post content items.

5
00:00:17,970 --> 00:00:22,556
A Read a data source step appears on the canvas, and its settings open:

6
00:00:22,556 --> 00:00:26,369
the fields to read, filters, and a maximum number of rows.

7
00:00:26,829 --> 00:00:30,819
In the Data tab, Preview runs the pipeline up to this step, on

8
00:00:30,819 --> 00:00:32,403
a sample of the rows.

9
00:00:32,403 --> 00:00:35,689
The Fields tab lists the fields the step produces.

10
00:00:36,163 --> 00:00:40,177
To add the next step, click the output port on the right edge of the step.

11
00:00:40,177 --> 00:00:42,887
The list offers the steps that can receive rows.

12
00:00:42,887 --> 00:00:44,543
Let's choose Create a file.

13
00:00:45,006 --> 00:00:47,720
Create a file writes the rows to a file.

14
00:00:47,720 --> 00:00:52,027
Its settings hold the format, which is CSV by default, and the file

15
00:00:52,027 --> 00:00:54,033
name, without its extension.

16
00:00:54,033 --> 00:00:56,216
Let's name the file blog-posts.

17
00:00:56,668 --> 00:00:58,443
A file has to go somewhere.

18
00:00:58,443 --> 00:01:02,906
Click the output port of Create a file, and choose Save to the media library.

19
00:01:02,906 --> 00:01:07,368
Its settings name a folder, and whether to replace a file with the same name.

